WHY A COPY IS HERE

check_oracle.py compiles the firmware's PinSketch against oracle.cpp and diffs it against
pinsketch.py case for case. That check could only run where a firmware checkout was reachable,
which is a developer's machine and not CI, so the one thing standing between the simulator and
sketches the firmware would never put on the air ran rarely and by hand.

WHAT THE COPY COSTS, AND WHAT PAYS FOR IT

A copy can drift, and a drifted copy is worse than no copy at all: the oracle would compile against
this directory, agree with pinsketch.py, and report 628 passing checks that prove only that two
files in this repository agree with each other. The cross-repository guarantee would have become a
tautology without anything failing.

So the firmware still wins whenever it is reachable. check_oracle prefers a real checkout, uses
this only as a fallback, and when both are present diffs them and fails on any difference. The copy
is what makes the check runnable everywhere; the firmware is still what makes it mean something.

REFRESHING

    cp $MESHTASTIC_FIRMWARE_ROOT/src/modules/Native/PinSketch.{h,cpp} sfpp/native/
    sha256sum sfpp/native/PinSketch.cpp sfpp/native/PinSketch.h   # update the hashes above
    git -C $MESHTASTIC_FIRMWARE_ROOT log -1 --format='%H %ad %s' --date=short -- \
        src/modules/Native/PinSketch.cpp                          # update commit/dated/subject

Then run `MESHTASTIC_FIRMWARE_ROOT=... python3 -m sfpp.check_oracle --require`, which is what
proves the refreshed copy still agrees with pinsketch.py.
